{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":3,"output":811,"cacheRead":9087,"cacheWrite":257,"reasoning":265,"totalTokens":10158,"cost":{"input":0.000007500000000000001,"output":0.012165,"cacheRead":0.00227175,"cacheWrite":0.000803125,"total":0.015247375}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":0,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_HivJomxZ9VABH3Q4a5JQZbXQ","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/INCIDENT.md","content":"# Event projector incident\n\nStatus: repaired and verified\n\n## Impact\n\nA temporary `applyToStore` rejection left the single promise chain rejected, so every later projection failed without running. Because that chain was shared by all trade IDs, a slow or failed persistence operation for one trade also blocked unrelated trades. In addition, state was published before persistence completed, allowing failed writes to appear in snapshots. The projector did not enforce event versions, lifecycle rules, payload validity, or complete mutation isolation.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe implementation used one global `pending` promise and replaced it with `pending.then(...)` for every event. There was no rejection recovery on that scheduling tail. It also called `states.set` before awaiting `applyToStore`, and `reduceEvent` accepted events without validating their shape, sequence, or terminal state.\n\n## Fix\n\nThe projector now maintains an independent promise tail per trade ID. Each operation waits only for earlier work on that trade, while the internal scheduling tail converts rejection to fulfillment so subsequent operations can continue; callers still receive the original rejection. Candidate state is validated and built inside that per-trade operation, persisted using cloned callback arguments, and committed only after persistence succeeds.\n\nThe repair also enforces first-event, exact-version, stale/duplicate, cancellation-terminal, supported-type, and payload rules. Events are cloned when accepted, and callback arguments, projection results, and snapshots are independently cloned so external mutation cannot change committed state. Settled queue entries are removed when they remain the latest tail.\n\n## Regression coverage\n\n`student-tests.mjs` adds six regression tests covering:\n\n- cross-trade progress while another trade's persistence is pending;\n- recovery and commit-after-success after a persistence rejection;\n- stale events, version gaps, and terminal cancellation;\n- invalid first events, malformed payloads, and unsupported types;\n- valid partial amendments retaining complete state; and\n- mutation isolation for input events, callback arguments, returned states, and snapshots.\n\nThe two committed tests continue to cover same-trade serialization and basic persistence-rejection recovery.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\nThe fixture uses in-memory state and per-process promise queues; it does not coordinate multiple projector instances or provide durable recovery if the process exits after the external store succeeds but before the in-memory commit. Those distributed/durability concerns are outside this fixture's contract. No known contract-level issue remains after the visible suite passed (8 tests, 0 failures).\n"}}}}
